n to hire Texas A&M's Trev Alberts as athletic director","description":"Michigan has reached a deal to hire Texas A&M athletic director Trev Alberts to replace the departing Warde Manuel, sources told ESPN on Tuesday.","published":"2026-11-18T05:46:35.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-sports/story/_/id/50516550/michigan-hire-texas-trev-alberts-athletic-director","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Pete Thamel and Dan Wetzel","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Who's on College Football Playoff bubble after the committee's third ranking?","description":"A conference-by-conference look at which teams are contending for the playoff, who's close and who's out.","published":"2026-11-18T03:42:45.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50503628/cfp-playoff-2026-bubble-watch-week-third-ranking","type":"Story","byline":"Heather Dinich","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Who's on the move? CFP committee's third top 25 and bracket of 2026","description":"A look at the new bracket based on the College Football Playoff committee's third set of rankings.","published":"2026-11-18T04:34:25.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50514170/cfp-2026-third-committee-top-25-bracket","type":"Story","byline":"ESPN","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Wake Forest surges into CFP top 12; top 4 unchanged while Texas jumps Notre Dame","description":"Alabama stayed at No. 3 despite some struggles on Saturday while two-loss Texas jumped one-loss Notre Dame for No. 5 in the CFP's latest rankings.","published":"2026-11-18T04:55:09.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50515993/wake-forest-surges-cfp-top-12-texas-jumps-notre-dame","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Mark Schlabach","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Pawn shop fighting 'Gold Digger' trademark filed by Jordon Hudson's company","description":"A California pawn shop is opposing a trademark application for \"Gold Digger\" filed by a company connected to Jordan Hudson, then-companion of Bill Belichick.","published":"2026-11-18T00:07:30.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/espn/story/_/id/50514442/pawn-shop-fights-gold-digger-trademark-filed-hudson","type":"HeadlineNews","byline":"Anthony Olivieri","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"Oklahoma coach Venables shouts out Love's after Buc-ee's comment","description":"Venables showed love Sunday to Buc-ee's -- which is owned by an alumnus of the Sooners' next opponent, Texas A&M. So on Tuesday the coach repped the Oklahoma-based Love's.","published":"2026-11-17T22:23:36.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50513199/oklahoma-brent-venables-loves-travel-stop","type":"Story","byline":"J.J. Post","source":"ESPN"},{"headline":"2026 college football coaching carousel: Tracking every open job","description":"As the carousel spins, we'll keep you updated with all the comings and goings.","published":"2026-11-17T21:26:31.000+00:00","url":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/story/_/id/50358611/2026-cfb-coaching-carousel-firings-hirings","type":"Story","byline":"ESPN","source":"ESPN"}],"links":{"espn":"https://www.espn.com/college-football/player/_/id/5218633/ryan-wingo","wikipedia":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryan_Wingo"},"wiki":{"rules":4,"title":"Ryan Wingo","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ryan_Wingo","description":"American football player (born 2006)","touched":"2026-11-14T07:36:23Z","intro":"Ryan Wingo (born February 15, 2006) is an American college football wide receiver for the Texas Longhorns.","sections":[{"key":"early","title":"Early life","text":"Wingo attended St. Louis University High School in St. Louis, Missouri. During his high school career he had 129 receptions for 2,160 yards and 31 receiving touchdowns. Wingo played in the 2024 All-American Bowl. A five-star recruit, he committed to the University of Texas at Austin to play college football."},{"key":"college","title":"College career","text":"In his first collegiate game as a true freshman in 2024, Wingo had four receptions for 70 yards. Wingo scored his first college touchdown against UTSA in Week 3. He played in all 16 games and ended the season with 472 receiving yards, 100 rushing yards, and 2 receiving touchdowns.\nIn his sophomore season in 2025, Wingo was named to the preseason All-SEC second team. He scored his first touchdown of the season in Week 3 against UTEP. In Week 4 against Sam Houston, Wingo logged 93 receiving yards, 32 rushing yards, and 2 touchdowns, making it his first game with multiple touchdowns. In Week 9 against Mississippi State, Wingo recorded a career-high 184 receiving yards on five receptions. At the end of the season, Wingo was named to the All-SEC second team."}],"family":[{"relation":"Sibling","name":"Ronnie Wingo","description":"American football player","url":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Ronnie_Wingo"}]}}